and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Nebraska remains one of the most reliably Republican states in presidential elections, with a partisan lean of roughly ten points toward the GOP. The race is open, with the incumbent term-limited, so neither side benefits from incumbency. Headlines show Republican figures such as JD Vance campaigning just across the river in Council Bluffs, while Democrats watch the state's split electoral allocation, but none of this amounts to a Nebraska-specific signal about the statewide winner.\n\nWith no polls, expert ratings, or market prices available for this race, the picture rests on fundamentals, which point to a Republican win in the neighborhood of ten points. The wide uncertainty band reflects the absence of direct polling rather than any sign of a competitive contest. A strong Democratic nominee or a major national shift could narrow the gap, but the structural lean is difficult to overcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1258, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1632, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1797, "margin": -10.0}]}